text:
This past week’s
@nytimes
Instagram launch, seven projects on the theme “Beginnings,” concludes today with three portraits by Newsha Tavakolian (
@newshatavakolian
), a photographer in Iran whose work will be featured soon on NYTimes.com.
The theme inspired the Times staff photographer Todd Heisler (
@heislerphoto
) to tell the story of an Iraqi refugee family adjusting to life in New York. Waleed Al Shok moved to the Bronx a year ago with his family, fleeing Baghdad because of the work he had been doing for the United States military. One year later, the family’s new beginning isn’t over.
“A year is not that long,” Mr. Heisler
said last week
. “There are some things that are still fresh for them.”
Instagram users were moved by photos of the Al Shok children — an energetic and happy pair, Mr. Heisler said. One Iraqi living in the city left a comment asking to be put in touch with the family to offer them support.
Some of the projects were more conceptual. We published a collection of images that Doug Mills (
@nytmills
), a staff photographer based in Washington, shot while he was, well, waiting around.
When Mr. Mills is on the road with President Obama, he spends much of his time waiting for events — or flights, or photo ops — to begin. It’s in those moments that Mr. Mills has a rare and contrasting view between the public space, where crowds stand waiting to catch a glimpse, and the presidential space, which is highly secure, highly orchestrated and often highly visual.
Photographs by Adam Ferguson (
@adamfergusonphoto
), Sim Chi Yin (
@chiyin_sim
), Daniel Berehulak (
@danielberehulak
) and Bryan Denton (
@bdentonphoto
) continue to resonate with Instagram users — in particular
the photo
by Mr. Denton we posted to begin our account, showing a woman leaning over to cuddle with a baby.
Starting Monday, the account will feature work by New York Times staff and freelance photographers, focusing on our strongest images with some insight into how they were made. Thanks for following.
